  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "r0lxc1AL/BrIoTEV8yk06d+UJBM=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-13T14:33:10.364Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "## The enforcement surface\n\nAll of these are public methods on `BaseSubgraph`. The `ph generate subgraph` scaffold hands you the instance as the `subgraph` parameter of `getResolvers`, so you call them as `subgraph.assertCanRead(...)`. Class-field resolvers call them on `this` instead \u2014 that is the style the built-in subgraphs use, and both forms reach the same public methods.\n\n| Helper | Use when | On failure |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `assertCanRead(identifier, ctx)` | Reading one document the caller named (id or slug) | throws `Forbidden` |\n| `assertCanWrite(identifier, ctx)` | Mutating one document the caller named | throws `Forbidden` |\n| `assertCanExecuteOperation(identifier, type, ctx)` | Executing a specific operation type on a document | throws `Forbidden` |\n| `assertCanCreate(ctx)` | Creating a genuinely top-level document with no parent | throws `Forbidden` |\n| `canReadDocument(canonicalId, ctx)` | Filtering a list \u2014 non-throwing read check on an id from the data layer | returns `boolean` |\n| `authorizationService.isSupremeAdmin(address)` | Short-circuiting per-item filtering for policy-wide callers | returns `boolean` |\n\nNote the sixth row: `isSupremeAdmin` is reached through `subgraph.authorizationService`, not directly on the subgraph.\n\n## Details worth knowing\n\nEvery `assertCan*` that takes an `identifier` returns an `AuthorizedDocumentHandle`; use its `fetchIdentifier` for the subsequent fetch. There are also lower-level `*Canonical` variants (`assertCanReadCanonical` and friends) used by the built-in subgraphs when the data layer has already resolved an id \u2014 prefer the identifier-accepting variants in your own code.\n\n`assertCanCreate` is narrower than it looks. When creating a document **under a parent**, the meaningful check is write access on that parent, so `assertCanWrite(parentIdentifier, ctx)` is the right call; reserve `assertCanCreate` for top-level documents.\n\nSupporting types exported from `@powerhousedao/reactor-api`: `Context`, `CanonicalDocumentId`, `AuthorizationPolicy`, `AuthorizedDocumentHandle`, `IAuthorizationService`.\n\n_Source: apps/academy/docs/academy/03-Build/03-BuildingUserExperiences/07-Authorization/05-EnforcingAuthorizationInSubgraphs.md (Powerhouse v6.2.2-dev.45)._",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-13T14:33:09.000Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-13T14:33:10.364Z"
    }
  },
